1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,050
What if your AI never had to search?

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:08,713
Cache-augmented generation, CAG, loads a
whole knowledge base into the model

3
00:00:08,713 --> 00:00:13,691
once, keeps the model's memory of it, and
answers every question without a

4
00:00:13,691 --> 00:00:14,700
retrieval step.

5
00:00:15,050 --> 00:00:19,300
I tested it against RAG on my own machine,
with real numbers.

6
00:00:19,650 --> 00:00:21,075
It works.

7
00:00:21,425 --> 00:00:24,425
But what decided whether it works wasn't the
trick.

8
00:00:24,775 --> 00:00:26,925
It was the size of the model.

9
00:00:27,567 --> 00:00:31,617
RAG looks up a few relevant passages for
every question.

10
00:00:31,967 --> 00:00:34,042
CAG skips the lookup.

11
00:00:34,392 --> 00:00:38,960
You put the entire knowledge base into the
prompt once, and the model

12
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:42,667
computes its internal memory of that text,
the KV cache.

13
00:00:43,017 --> 00:00:48,168
Every later question reuses that memory, so
only the question itself has to

14
00:00:48,168 --> 00:00:48,717
be read.

15
00:00:49,067 --> 00:00:54,083
The catch: everything must fit in the
model's context window, everyone gets

16
00:00:54,083 --> 00:00:57,092
the same text, and the first request is
slow.

17
00:00:57,442 --> 00:01:01,917
It suits a small, stable knowledge base that
people ask about a lot.

18
00:01:02,528 --> 00:01:04,603
Everything here is local.

19
00:01:04,953 --> 00:01:10,281
Ollama, a Qwen eight-billion model and a
Qwen thirty-two-billion model, on

20
00:01:10,281 --> 00:01:12,153
one MacBook, no cloud API.

21
00:01:12,503 --> 00:01:17,524
The knowledge base is a slice of Ollama's
own documentation: a hundred and

22
00:01:17,524 --> 00:01:21,053
eighteen sections, about twenty-six thousand
tokens.

23
00:01:21,403 --> 00:01:26,084
I built this with an AI coding assistant,
and it wrote the ninety test

24
00:01:26,084 --> 00:01:26,753
questions.

25
00:01:27,103 --> 00:01:31,898
Fifty-one of them ask about things the
documentation never says, because the

26
00:01:31,898 --> 00:01:35,178
right answer to those is: not in the
knowledge base.

27
00:01:35,528 --> 00:01:39,053
Forty questions for development, fifty held
back.

28
00:01:39,403 --> 00:01:41,428
No human reviewed them.

29
00:01:41,778 --> 00:01:43,603
I'll come back to that.

30
00:01:44,230 --> 00:01:47,255
First question: does the cache actually
work?

31
00:01:47,805 --> 00:01:51,330
Here is the eight-billion model on the
documentation.

32
00:01:51,680 --> 00:01:56,198
The first call has to read all twenty-six
thousand tokens, which took me

33
00:01:56,198 --> 00:01:58,080
between two and three minutes.

34
00:01:58,430 --> 00:02:02,533
After that, reading the prompt takes a
fraction of a second, because more

35
00:02:02,533 --> 00:02:05,455
than ninety-nine percent of it comes from
the cache.

36
00:02:05,805 --> 00:02:10,170
With the thirty-two-billion model that first
call takes eight and a half

37
00:02:10,170 --> 00:02:10,655
minutes.

38
00:02:11,005 --> 00:02:14,480
That cold start is the real price of CAG.

39
00:02:15,095 --> 00:02:16,895
My first surprise.

40
00:02:17,245 --> 00:02:22,652
The cache lives inside Ollama, and one
Ollama server has exactly one slot

41
00:02:22,652 --> 00:02:23,170
for it.

42
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:28,213
When I loaded a second model for chat, it
pushed the cache out, and the next

43
00:02:28,213 --> 00:02:32,720
question paid the full cold start again,
sixty-five seconds in that test.

44
00:02:33,070 --> 00:02:37,120
The fix was a dedicated Ollama server for
the cached model.

45
00:02:37,746 --> 00:02:41,096
Now the result, on the fifty held-out
questions.

46
00:02:41,446 --> 00:02:45,796
The eight-billion model with CAG got twenty-
eight percent right.

47
00:02:46,146 --> 00:02:50,634
It answered thirty-six of the forty
questions the documentation can't

48
00:02:50,634 --> 00:02:54,471
answer, inventing details instead of saying
it didn't know.

49
00:02:54,821 --> 00:02:57,971
RAG with the same small model got eighty-
four.

50
00:02:58,321 --> 00:03:03,303
The thirty-two-billion model with CAG got
ninety-eight percent, refused all

51
00:03:03,303 --> 00:03:08,153
forty unanswerable questions, and answered
in three seconds, where RAG on

52
00:03:08,153 --> 00:03:10,146
the same model takes nineteen.

53
00:03:10,496 --> 00:03:12,521
Same trick, same data.

54
00:03:12,871 --> 00:03:15,621
The difference was the size of the model.

55
00:03:16,248 --> 00:03:21,073
I tried to make answers traceable by telling
the model to quote its sources.

56
00:03:21,423 --> 00:03:25,473
Citations went from five percent to sixty-
five percent.

57
00:03:25,823 --> 00:03:29,148
But the small model started inventing
evidence.

58
00:03:29,498 --> 00:03:34,645
Asked about mutual TLS, which the
documentation never mentions, it wrote a

59
00:03:34,645 --> 00:03:39,723
sentence that isn't in the documentation and
pinned it on a real section.

60
00:03:40,073 --> 00:03:42,298
So I stopped asking the model.

61
00:03:42,648 --> 00:03:47,137
After it answers, I find the supporting
section by matching the answer

62
00:03:47,137 --> 00:03:48,548
against the documents.

63
00:03:48,898 --> 00:03:53,498
That found the right section ninety-three to
a hundred percent of the time.

64
00:03:54,110 --> 00:03:57,260
The next surprise was about my own
measurements.

65
00:03:57,610 --> 00:04:02,494
On the first forty questions, a semantic
similarity check looked like the

66
00:04:02,494 --> 00:04:04,835
best way to catch invented answers.

67
00:04:05,185 --> 00:04:09,868
Then I wrote fifty new questions, and it
flipped: the simple word-overlap

68
00:04:09,868 --> 00:04:10,510
check won.

69
00:04:10,860 --> 00:04:14,260
Forty questions had been too few to choose a
method.

70
00:04:14,610 --> 00:04:19,370
The small model's refusal rate told the same
story: twenty-seven percent on

71
00:04:19,370 --> 00:04:22,035
the first set, ten percent on the new one.

72
00:04:22,385 --> 00:04:25,210
Always keep questions you haven't tuned on.

73
00:04:25,828 --> 00:04:29,153
A production service has to survive
failures.

74
00:04:29,503 --> 00:04:32,653
So I killed Ollama in the middle of a
request.

75
00:04:33,003 --> 00:04:36,228
The caller got an error in under a tenth of
a second.

76
00:04:36,578 --> 00:04:40,749
The health check stayed up, readiness
switched to not ready, and when I

77
00:04:40,749 --> 00:04:45,155
restarted Ollama the service re-read the
whole knowledge base by itself and

78
00:04:45,155 --> 00:04:48,503
recovered, in two and a half minutes for the
small model.

79
00:04:48,853 --> 00:04:52,983
For the big one, just re-reading takes eight
and a half minutes, so you

80
00:04:52,983 --> 00:04:54,903
would want a second copy running.

81
00:04:55,519 --> 00:04:57,244
Then I attacked it.

82
00:04:57,594 --> 00:05:00,669
I hid instructions inside the documents.

83
00:05:01,019 --> 00:05:05,600
Neither model obeyed a command like ignore
your rules, but the bigger one

84
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:09,993
followed a hidden instruction once and added
a tracking link, and both

85
00:05:09,993 --> 00:05:13,194
repeated a false fact I planted as if it
were true.

86
00:05:13,544 --> 00:05:18,319
No prompt fixes that: you have to control
what goes into the knowledge base.

87
00:05:18,669 --> 00:05:24,143
A test asking for planted emails, card
numbers and keys got all five back,

88
00:05:24,143 --> 00:05:25,919
until I added redaction.

89
00:05:26,269 --> 00:05:31,236
And under load the service handles about
five requests a minute however many

90
00:05:31,236 --> 00:05:35,419
people ask, so now it turns the extras away
in ten milliseconds.

91
00:05:36,048 --> 00:05:38,723
Last, I wanted to catch regressions.

92
00:05:39,073 --> 00:05:44,326
A gate re-runs the questions and fails if
accuracy or abstention drops below

93
00:05:44,326 --> 00:05:47,298
thresholds taken from the held-out results.

94
00:05:47,648 --> 00:05:52,348
It also locks the knowledge base and the
prompt: change one fact in the

95
00:05:52,348 --> 00:05:57,048
documentation and the check fails until you
re-run the full evaluation.

96
00:05:57,398 --> 00:06:01,523
It runs in seconds, and a secret scanner
checks every push.

97
00:06:02,134 --> 00:06:03,784
Five things.

98
00:06:04,134 --> 00:06:09,509
One: CAG fits a small, stable knowledge base
that gets asked about a lot.

99
00:06:09,859 --> 00:06:13,809
Two: the cache is the product, so budget for
the cold start.

100
00:06:14,159 --> 00:06:17,884
Three: model size mattered more than any
trick I tried.

101
00:06:18,234 --> 00:06:22,634
Four: measure what happens on questions the
knowledge base can't answer.

102
00:06:22,984 --> 00:06:25,759
Five: keep questions you didn't tune on.

103
00:06:26,109 --> 00:06:31,172
Everything here is local and open, with the
caveats stated: one machine,

104
00:06:31,172 --> 00:06:34,759
ninety questions written by an AI, no human
review.
